dwmapi, win32u, winewayland: DwmEnableBlurBehindWindow composites the window with its pixels' alpha, as on Windows.

CapCut 9.7 (Qt 6.2, lab, 2026-11-03, Wayland driver): opening any of its menus turned the whole screen black except the
menu. Its menus, its floating EditPilot button and its home window are frameless translucent Qt windows drawn with
Direct3D 11: WS_EX_LAYERED with SetLayeredWindowAttributes(LWA_ALPHA, 255), a blt-model (DXGI_SWAP_EFFECT_DISCARD) RGBA
swap chain, and DwmEnableBlurBehindWindow with an empty blur region, which is how Qt makes such a window translucent: once
blur-behind is on, Windows' compositor blends the window by its pixels' alpha, and blt-model presents land in that window
surface. A menu is a popup the size of the whole screen, transparent but for the menu. Wine's DwmEnableBlurBehindWindow
was a stub (E_NOTIMPL), the layered window surface was forced opaque (alpha 255 OR-ed in), and DXVK presented opaque, so
the transparent part came out black over everything.

DwmEnableBlurBehindWindow(DWM_BB_ENABLE) now sets a flag on the window (NtUserCallHwndParam_SetDwmAlpha, win32u). win32u
copies it to every surface of the window (a DPI-scaled wrapper's target too), and winewayland then composites that
surface with its pixels' alpha, the way it already does per-pixel layered surfaces: ARGB buffer, alpha passed through, GDI
pixels (alpha 0 with a color) kept opaque. The window keeps being hit-tested as a whole, as on Windows. dwmapi also marks
the window __wine_dwm_alpha, which DXVK's presenter reads (external dxvk 0006): a blt-model swap chain presenting into such
a window gets VK_COMPOSITE_ALPHA_PRE_MULTIPLIED_BIT_KHR when the surface supports it.

Not covered: the blur itself; a constant layered alpha below 255 on top of the pixels' alpha; a window that enables
blur-behind after its swap chain exists (DXVK reads the mark when it creates the swap chain); DwmExtendFrameIntoClientArea
(still a stub). Kill switch NEUTRON_DWM_ALPHA=0 (dwmapi returns E_NOTIMPL as before; DXVK's half then never fires).

Lab, CapCut 9.7 on 176 + this + dxvk 0006 (PgI-capcut): Menu opens over the editor, the editor visible around it, 3 of 3
(submenus too); the EditPilot button loses its black box. NEUTRON_DWM_ALPHA=0 on the same build: black, 1 of 1 (and
black on 175t, 2 of 2); NEUTRON_DXVK_PREMULTIPLIED=0: black, 1 of 1. Photoshop 2026 (with an earlier 0006 that also
premultiplied composition swap chains, since dropped as unproven): New Document typed name + document created, captures
unchanged. Filmora 16 makes no blur-behind calls (start-up traced).


--- a/dlls/dwmapi/dwmapi_main.c
+++ b/dlls/dwmapi/dwmapi_main.c
@@ -28,6 +28,7 @@
 #include "wingdi.h"
 #include "winuser.h"
 #include "dwmapi.h"
+#include "ntuser.h"
 #include "wine/debug.h"
 
 WINE_DEFAULT_DEBUG_CHANNEL(dwmapi);
@@ -162,9 +163,31 @@
  */
 HRESULT WINAPI DwmEnableBlurBehindWindow(HWND hWnd, const DWM_BLURBEHIND *pBlurBuf)
 {
-    FIXME("%p %p\n", hWnd, pBlurBuf);
+    char gate[4] = {0};
 
-    return E_NOTIMPL;
+    TRACE("%p %p\n", hWnd, pBlurBuf);
+
+    if (!pBlurBuf) return E_INVALIDARG;
+    if (GetEnvironmentVariableA("NEUTRON_DWM_ALPHA", gate, sizeof(gate)) && gate[0] == '0')
+    {
+        FIXME("%p %p, NEUTRON_DWM_ALPHA=0\n", hWnd, pBlurBuf);
+        return E_NOTIMPL;
+    }
+
+    /* NEUTRON dwm-alpha: the blur itself is not drawn, but the window is composited with its pixels'
+     * alpha, which is what translucent GPU-drawn windows enable it for (Qt: an empty blur region).
+     * win32u composites the window surface that way; the __wine_dwm_alpha mark tells DXVK that a
+     * blt-model swap chain presenting into this window should keep its alpha too (on Windows those
+     * presents land in the window surface DWM blends). */
+    if (pBlurBuf->dwFlags & DWM_BB_ENABLE)
+    {
+        TRACE("enable %d flags %#lx\n", pBlurBuf->fEnable, pBlurBuf->dwFlags);
+        if (!NtUserCallHwndParam( hWnd, !!pBlurBuf->fEnable, NtUserCallHwndParam_SetDwmAlpha ))
+            return E_HANDLE;
+        if (pBlurBuf->fEnable) SetPropW( hWnd, L"__wine_dwm_alpha", (HANDLE)1 );
+        else RemovePropW( hWnd, L"__wine_dwm_alpha" );
+    }
+    return S_OK;
 }
 
 /**********************************************************************
--- a/dlls/dwmapi/Makefile.in
+++ b/dlls/dwmapi/Makefile.in
@@ -1,5 +1,5 @@
 MODULE    = dwmapi.dll
-IMPORTS   = user32
+IMPORTS   = user32 win32u
 IMPORTLIB = dwmapi
 
 VER_FILEDESCRIPTION_STR = "Desktop Window Manager API"
--- a/dlls/win32u/dce.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/dce.c
@@ -330,6 +330,9 @@
         window_surface_set_layered( surface->target_surface, window_surface->color_key,
                                     window_surface->alpha_bits, window_surface->alpha_mask );
     }
+    /* NEUTRON dwm-alpha: the target composites the same way */
+    if (surface->target_surface->dwm_alpha != window_surface->dwm_alpha)
+        window_surface_set_dwm_alpha( surface->target_surface, window_surface->dwm_alpha );
 
     /* NEUTRON shape-hittest: this wrapper reported the shape in the window's dpi; the target's is device pixels */
     flush_surface( surface->target_surface, FALSE );
@@ -1145,6 +1148,19 @@
     }
     window_surface_unlock( surface );
 }
+
+/* NEUTRON dwm-alpha: composite the surface with its pixels' alpha (DwmEnableBlurBehindWindow). */
+void window_surface_set_dwm_alpha( struct window_surface *surface, BOOL enable )
+{
+    if (surface == &dummy_surface) return;
+    window_surface_lock( surface );
+    if (surface->dwm_alpha != enable)
+    {
+        surface->dwm_alpha = enable;
+        surface->bounds = surface->rect;
+    }
+    window_surface_unlock( surface );
+}
 
 void window_surface_set_clip( struct window_surface *surface, HRGN clip_region )
 {
